1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,800
This is a photograph of tartan. Beside it is a draft showing how to weave its repeating structure.

2
00:00:04,800 --> 00:00:10,200
I built ThreadLore to help a weaver get from one to the other.

3
00:00:10,410 --> 00:00:15,500
Imagine you're documenting a textile whose weaving instructions have been lost.

4
00:00:15,500 --> 00:00:19,200
The photograph preserves the colors and checks.

5
00:00:19,200 --> 00:00:23,700
To set up a loom, you also need to know which yarn passes over the other at each crossing.

6
00:00:23,730 --> 00:00:28,500
I'll start with this tartan, choosing a patch where I can follow individual yarns.

7
00:00:28,500 --> 00:00:32,500
When I click Confirm and Analyze, watch the loading indicator.

8
00:00:32,500 --> 00:00:39,700
ThreadLore is analyzing optical thread density, matching warp and weft yarn colors, and reconstructing the weave structure.

9
00:00:39,740 --> 00:00:45,200
Here's the analysis. The engine matched the yarn colors and labeled this as two-over, two-under twill, so I'll use that as my starting point.

10
00:00:45,200 --> 00:00:51,500
Now I want to see whether those crossings line up with the photograph.

11
00:00:51,520 --> 00:00:57,500
Watch this yarn near the middle. I'll slide the grid until the intersection sits over the crossing I'm looking at.

12
00:00:57,500 --> 00:01:03,900
Now I can follow the yarn across the photo and compare the next few crossings with the proposed weave.

13
00:01:03,930 --> 00:01:10,200
Now watch the photo while I change one square on the right. That crossing flips everywhere the tile repeats across the fabric.

14
00:01:10,200 --> 00:01:17,700
This whole design has just sixteen crossings to review. I'll flip that square back before compiling.

15
00:01:17,780 --> 00:01:21,500
I can give the swatch a title and record what I know about it.

16
00:01:21,500 --> 00:01:24,000
I'll leave the maker and date blank because I don't know them.

17
00:01:24,020 --> 00:01:31,000
Once I approve the tile, the compiler works out which shaft carries each thread, which shafts lift together, and which treadle to press for each row.

18
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:37,700
Then it simulates those instructions and checks the crossings against the tile I approved.

19
00:01:37,720 --> 00:01:43,500
Here's the sheet a weaver can take to the loom. It puts the setup instructions beside a preview of the fabric.

20
00:01:43,500 --> 00:01:48,600
I can also export the draft as a WIF file for weaving software.

21
00:01:48,640 --> 00:01:54,500
I also tested a photograph documented as plain weave. ThreadLore identified its two-by-two repeat.

22
00:01:54,500 --> 00:02:05,500
Reading the exported loom instructions back independently reproduced the expected over-under pattern at all 256 crossings in this test.

23
00:02:05,510 --> 00:02:11,500
The first request starts here, with the cropped photo.

24
00:02:11,500 --> 00:02:16,050
React sends it to FastAPI on Cloud Run, where OpenCV estimates the spacing and angle of the yarns.

25
00:02:16,050 --> 00:02:20,500
Gemini Flash on Vertex AI suggests a weave.

26
00:02:20,500 --> 00:02:27,140
Back in the browser, the weaver can align the overlay with the photo and correct individual crossings in the tile.

27
00:02:27,140 --> 00:02:31,000
The second request sends that approved tile to the loom compiler. It calculates the loom setup.

28
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:38,660
The validator simulates those instructions and checks every crossing against the tile.

29
00:02:38,660 --> 00:02:46,240
The result is a four-part draft to inspect, a WIF file for weaving software, and a printable card to take to the loom.

